<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Septennial Act 1716</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Septennial Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Septennial Act 1716 was a law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-great-britain">Parliament of Great Britain</a> that extended the maximum life of a parliament from three to seven years. Passed by the Whig government soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Jacobite rising of 1715</a>, it postponed an election the Whigs feared to lose and helped them keep power for decades. The limit remained until the Parliament Act 1911 reduced it to five years.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The act, passed in May 1716, was very short. It provided that the sitting Parliament and every later one should continue for seven years and no longer, counted from the day appointed for its meeting in the writ of summons, unless the monarch dissolved it sooner. In doing so it overturned provisions of the Triennial Act 1694, passed by the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which had set the limit at three years. The law fixed only a ceiling and did not require a parliament to run its full term.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Its stated aim was to reduce the expense of frequent elections, but it may also have kept in power the Whig party, which had won the general election of 1715 and won again in 1722. Most parliaments of the rest of the 18th century lasted six or seven years, only two ending sooner, while in the 19th century the average term of the Parliament of the United Kingdom was four years. Annually elected parliaments were the one demand of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartists</a> still not achieved by the 20th century.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> a series of acts prolonged the parliament elected in December 1910 until the end of the war in 1918, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> annual acts kept the parliament elected in 1935 sitting until the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> ended in mid-1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a> criticised the act, as did Henry St John, 1st Viscount Bolingbroke, who wrote in his Dissertation upon Parties that the &quot;constitution is the rule by which our princes ought to govern at all times&quot;. In Federalist No. 53 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> used it to show the difference between a constitution established by the people and unalterable by the government and a law the government could change. Section 7 of the Parliament Act 1911 cut the limit to five years on 18 August 1911. The Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 repealed the whole act, and its rule was re-enacted with minor differences as section 4 of the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Septennial Act 1715” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Septennial Act</b> of 1716 lengthened parliaments to seven years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
